usb: dwc2: split transactions in descriptor-DMA mode - tried, does not work (NOT APPLIED)

Diff from the shipped dwc2 state (patches/0026 applied to the baseline) to
the 2026-09-03 experiment. Kept for the record; the port tree is back to
the 0026 state. Apply with `patch -p1` in linux-71-port to repeat it.

What it does: lets control/bulk/interrupt split QHs into descriptor mode,
sets HCSPLT_SPLTENA on the descriptor start path (upstream only sets it
on the buffer path, so the first experiment addressed the full-speed
device directly at high speed and got XactErr - that result was void),
fixes dwc2_frame_incr_val() for split intervals (microframes, not
frames), derives HCTSIZ.SCHED_INFO for split periodic channels and
writes 0xff for non-periodic ones (Espressif's HAL found channels
freezing otherwise), keeps PING off splits, keeps descriptor DMA on at
host init for a high-speed root device (dma_desc_fs_enable only with
host_full_speed), keeps the buffer-DMA ACK/NAK/NYET handlers off
descriptor channels, adds dwc2.split_trace (count every response) and
dwc2.split_sw_csplit (drive the complete-split from software on a halt).

What happened, clean high-speed boot, Genesys hub at 480 Mbit/s, Logitech
and 8BitDo receivers behind it, SOF off, 0 USB irq/s, cpubench 70 M/s:
the first control transfer to a device behind the hub goes out as a
start-split and then NOTHING - no ACK, NYET, XactErr, halt or completion
interrupt, ever; the descriptor stays active and the request times out
(-110) 15 s later. The stuck channel then starves the controller: the
hub's own hub_ext_port_status requests time out too and USB is dead until
reset. The software complete-split fallback never engages because there
is no event to engage on. Conclusion: this core does not sequence split
transactions in Scatter/Gather DMA mode and gives software nothing to
sequence them with; the only working path for full/low-speed devices
behind a high-speed hub is buffer DMA with the 8 kHz SOF.

